    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2028 Mitsubishi Outlander Diamond Trail Edition: How Does It Compare to Other Adventure Crossovers?\nCanadian Gearhead Bought Survivor 18k-Mile HUMVEE Just To Be Cool at Car Meets, Did He Nail It?\n63 photos\nPhoto: YouTube/Classic Car ChitChat\nWhat the coolest military vehicle of all time? You can’t chose planes, ships, tanks, drones, submarines, helicopters, motorcycles, horses, hot air balloons, or anything along those lines. In fact, stick to four-wheel vehicles. The Willys Jeep? Mmmm… good one. In fact, great call, but how about something more contemporary? Like, say, its grandson, the HMMWV. Catchy military acronym, right? That’s why soldiers called it, in the most army-style jargon, the Humvee.\nWhen the United States military realized in 1979 that its aging fleet of tactical trucks could no longer keep pace with modern mechanized warfare, the Pentagon drafted specifications for a radical replacement. The High Mobility Multipurpose Wheeled Vehicle, universally known as the Humvee, was conceived to consolidate the roles of the M151 Jeep, the M561 Gama Goat, and various Dodge utility pickups into one single, unstoppable, all-terrain platform.\nAM General won the initial prototype contract in 1981 and began mass production at its Mishawaka, Indiana facility on January 2, 1985. Since then, over 281,000 units have rolled off the assembly line, serving in the 1989 invasion of Panama, the Persian Gulf War, Somalia, the Balkans, the War in Afghanistan, the Iraq War (both of them), and the Russo-Ukrainian War, while spawning the civilian Hummer H1 in 1992, followed by the H2, H3, and GMC Hummer EV.\nEngineered around a single shared chassis, the military workhorse accommodated 15 distinct configurations, including two-door and four-door troop and cargo carriers, weapons platforms, ambulances, shelter carriers, and TOW missile launchers. Early models relied on a naturally aspirated 6.2-liter Detroit Diesel V8 generating 150 hp (152 PS) and 250 lb-ft (339 Nm) of torque, paired with a 3-speed GM TH400/3L80 automatic transmission and a two-range New Process 218 transfer case.\nPhoto: YouTube/Classic Car ChitChat Starting in 1994, the upgraded A2 variants received a 6.5-liter Detroit Diesel V8 delivering 160 hp (162 PS) and 290 lb-ft (393 Nm) in naturally aspirated guise, or up to 190 hp (193 PS) and 385 lb-ft (522 Nm) when turbocharged, mated to a 4-speed 4L80-E automatic gearbox. Later GEP Optimizer 6500 turbo-diesels pushed output to 205 hp (208 PS) and 440 lb-ft (597 Nm) to handle heavy armor.\nWhile most surviving military units endured grueling front-line combat, one remarkably preserved 1988 AM General M998 Humvee escaped the battlefield to become an unlikely star on the Canadian car show circuit. Kevin Chauhan, the host of the Classic Car ChitChat YouTube channel, caught up with the truck and its proud owner, Darryl, in Ontario to document how an authentic military utility rig became a crowd-pleasing weekend cruiser.\nFinished in its original woodland Chemical Agent Resistant Coating applied in June 1999, this 1988 M998 spent its active-duty career stationed at a United States Air Force base in Florida, as evidenced by the stenciled markings on its split flat glass windshield. Because base logistics required only short perimeter trips rather than cross-country convoys, the odometer shows just 18,000 original miles (28,968 kilometers) accumulated over nearly four decades.\nPhoto: YouTube/Classic Car ChitChat After being decommissioned in June 1999, the truck sat in dry Florida storage before making its way north into Canada, where it was acquired by a professional working in the film industry. Darryl, a lifelong gearhead who started driving in a three-on-the-tree 1972 Ford F-100 and has owned over 25 pickup trucks, bought the Humvee from the movie prop specialist simply to be the coolest kid on the block. He absolutely nailed it.\nStepping up to the massive truck reveals why it steals the spotlight from shiny Corvettes at Oakville car meets. Built on a rigid boxed-steel frame with a 130-inch (3,302-millimeter) wheelbase and an 85-inch (2,159-millimeter) width, the M998 features a heat-treated riveted aluminum body paired with a heavy forward-tilting fiberglass hood. To save weight over its 5,200-pound (2,359-kilogram) curb mass, AM General fitted zippered canvas doors and a soft top.\nEvery exterior detail was dictated by battlefield survival and rapid deployment. Up front, a heavy-duty steel push guard secured by oversized hitch pins protects the seven-slot grille and upgraded headlights, flanked by hooded blackout marker lamps designed to prevent aerial detection at night. Massive steel lifting shackles bolted directly through the hood and rear bumper allow the entire 7,700-pound (3,493-kilogram) gross-weight vehicle to be airlifted beneath a military transport helicopter.\nPhoto: YouTube/Classic Car ChitChat Currently configured as a nine-passenger troop carrier with wooden slatted side racks and longitudinal bench seats in the bed, the truck carries two spare 37-inch Goodyear Wrangler military tires in the cargo area. Each wheel assembly houses a solid high-grade polyurethane run-flat insert capable of supporting the truck if the tires are punctured by gunfire, with the pair of spares alone weighing more than 500 pounds (227 kilograms).\nUnlatching the heavy fiberglass hood requires releasing two side clamps and folding down the front brush guard to expose the cavernous engine bay. Tucked far back behind a triple-stacked cooling radiator array sits the original 6.2-liter (379-cubic-inch) Detroit Diesel V8 engine, fed by a waterproof 24-volt electrical system with an external jump-start receptacle at the rear.\nThe drivetrain sits high between the frame rails to grant 16 inches (406 millimeters) of ground clearance, courtesy of portal axles that allow the tires to be inflated on the run, while the truck is rolling along.\nPhoto: YouTube/Classic Car ChitChat Inside the spartan cabin, a massive central tunnel (the size of a front lawn) houses the 3-speed automatic transmission and transfer case right between the seats, leaving room for an M16 rifle mount by the driver's left knee and a rotary ignition switch that requires no key. Despite lacking a rearview mirror, air conditioning, or sound deadening, the independent double-wishbone coil-spring suspension and portal gear hubs deliver a surprisingly composed ride over rough terrain.\nThe Humvee is one of those iconic war machines that are likely to never lose their cool, precisely due to its rhino stance and absolutely Zero-BS attitude. Yes, it’s got a face only a mother would love, but it was never meant to be a beauty pageant queen. After all, nobody buys a sledgehammer to put it on display in the living room. And this knight may be flashing a shining armor, but it also rocks a swagger no civilian vehicle can ever match. Ever.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHumvee AM General Humvee 1988 HUMVEE military truck pickup truck truck USAF classic car chitchat\nAbout the author: Razvan Calin\nAfter nearly two decades in news television, Răzvan turned to a different medium. He’s been a field journalist, a TV producer, and a seafarer but found that he feels right at home among petrolheads. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1966 Chevy Impala Convertible Has the Right Engine, Shipping Will Cost a Fortune",
